WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:02.100
YUFENG GUO: Data is messy.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.730
It's often unbalanced,
mislabeled,

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:08.039
and sprinkled with wacky values
that throw off your analysis

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:09.930
and machine learning training.

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.680
The first step to getting
your dataset cleaned up

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:15.630
is understanding where
it needs cleaning.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:19.500
Today, I've got just
the tool for the job.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.270
Understanding your
data is the first step

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:23.920
to getting your dataset cleaned
up for machine learning.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:26.010
But this can be tricky
to do, especially

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:28.860
in any sort of
generalizable manner.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.930
An open source project
from Google Research

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:34.140
called Facets can help
us visualize our data

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.330
and slice it in all
sorts of manners

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:40.050
which can help us begin to see
how our dataset is laid out.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:42.690
By allowing us to find
where our data doesn't quite

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:47.220
look the way we're expecting,
Facets helps reduce mishaps

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:48.540
down the road.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.220
Let's see what
Facets looks like.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.270
The team has a demo
page on the web

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:54.570
so you can try out
Facets from Chrome

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:56.820
without installing anything.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:01.230
Moreover, Facets visualizations
use Polymer Web Components

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:04.140
backed by a TypeScript
code so you can easily

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.210
embed this in web pages
and into Jupyter notebooks.

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:10.430
There are two parts to Facets--

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:14.060
Facets Overview and
Facets Deep Dive.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:18.120
Let's take a look at each
one now in more detail.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:23.090
Facets Overview gives-- well,
an overview of your dataset.

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:26.540
In previous videos, we
saw how tools like pandas

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:30.470
can help us get a sense of how
our dataset is distributed.

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:32.900
We can get a slightly
upgraded view

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:38.600
of this type of information
using the Facets Overview tool.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.840
Facets Overview splits
the columns of our data

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:43.700
into rows of
salient information,

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:48.590
showing stuff such as percentage
missing, min and max values,

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:52.730
as well as stats like mean,
median, and standard deviation.

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:55.610
It also has a column that shows
the percentage of values that

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:58.970
are zeros, which totally helps
with catching cases where

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:01.070
most of your values are zeros.

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:04.250
You can also see these
distributions on your test

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:07.910
dataset as well as your
training set for each feature.

00:02:07.910 --> 00:02:10.085
This way, you can
double-check that the test

00:02:10.085 --> 00:02:14.300
set has a similar distribution
to the training dataset.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.940
Yes, indeed,
technically this is best

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:20.540
practice to do at least this
level of analysis on your own

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:23.030
anyway, but I have
definitely forgotten

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:27.020
to check all of these aspects
of every column of my data.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:28.520
This tool will help
ensure that you

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:30.740
don't miss this
crucial step, and it

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:34.050
highlights some abnormalities.

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:37.160
Now let's take a look
at Facets Deep Dive.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.530
This is where things
get really interesting.

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:42.530
It allows you to have even
more clarity into your dataset

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:47.570
and zoom all the way in to see
an individual piece of data.

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:50.900
You can "facet" the
data by row and column

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:53.360
across any of the
features of your dataset.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:55.550
This will look
familiar to views when

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:59.010
you're shopping online
for, say, shoes, and filter

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:03.880
by different categories, such
as size, brand, and color.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:08.440
Let's take a look at a concrete
example of Deep Dive in action.

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:12.010
The interface is divided
up into four main sections.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:15.760
The main area in the center is
a zoomable display of your data.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:17.290
On the left-hand
panel is where you

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:19.420
can change the
arrangement of your data

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:21.190
with various
dropdowns to control

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:24.820
things like faceting,
positioning, and color.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:28.270
Directly below that is a
legend for the center display,

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:30.880
and on the far right
is a detailed view

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.040
of a specific row of data.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:36.430
You can click on any dot of
data in the center visualization

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:40.420
to see a detailed readout of
that particular data point.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:44.150
Now let's see how this
all comes together.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:46.720
To do this, we'll use
the "Census Dataset,"

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:51.130
a classic dataset extracted
from the 1994 US census

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:52.690
by Barry Becker.

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:55.870
Its target is to predict
whether a household's annual is

00:03:55.870 --> 00:04:03.290
above or below $50,000, based
on various census statistics.

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:06.240
We'll first split the data
by age range and color

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:08.730
the data points based
on the target value.

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:13.950
Here, blue means less than 50K,
and red means greater than 50K.

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:16.620
Now we can look at
the breakdown, by age,

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:19.230
of another feature of the data--

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:21.660
say, do different
number of hours per week

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:25.020
yield different results
across age ranges?

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:29.020
Let's find out by faceting
columns by hours per week.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:32.430
We can see that there is a
larger chunk of the age 17-26

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:37.020
population working 15 to 29
hours per week, which may just

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:40.490
be the result of kids doing
summer jobs, let's say.

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:43.680
And we can also see the trend of
fewer and fewer people working

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:49.470
29 to 43 hours per week as they
get older, while the 43 to 57

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:52.860
hour segment stays relatively
constant in the middle years

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:54.420
of the chart.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:56.580
But this still
doesn't quite show us

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:58.060
what we are looking for.

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:00.420
So let's try changing the
positioning of the plot

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:02.340
and getting a more
detailed look.

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:04.410
We'll switch the
positioning to "Scatter"

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:06.690
and only facet by age.

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:08.700
I'm also going to
select "Hours per Week"

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:10.800
as our vertical
sort order to make

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:15.390
it easy to see the working hours
across different age groups.

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:19.050
Now we can see that indeed,
the hours per week rise

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:24.260
in the middle of the chart
and lower on either side.

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:26.150
You should continue
exploring this data

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:28.440
and see what trends and
relationships you can find.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:30.860
For instance, you
might facet by country

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.539
of origin, which would show
that the data is heavily skewed,

00:05:34.539 --> 00:05:36.080
and this might tell
you that you need

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:40.930
to collect more data points to
have a more balanced dataset.

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:42.730
If this was interesting,
you're probably

00:05:42.730 --> 00:05:45.970
wondering how to get your
dataset loaded into Facets.

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:48.290
Well, here you have two options.

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:51.040
You can either use the web
interface and just upload

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:53.710
the data directly and play
with it in the browser,

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:57.970
or you can install the library
as a Jupyter notebook extension

00:05:57.970 --> 00:06:01.240
using the instructions on the
project's GitHub page, which

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:02.780
I'll link below.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:05.950
Facets is a useful tool
for peering into your data

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:08.650
and seeing the relationships
between different features,

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:11.470
as well as ensuring that
there aren't missing

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:15.020
or unexpected values
in your dataset.

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:18.340
Thanks for watching this
episode of Cloud AI Adventures.

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:20.080
Be sure to subscribe
to the channel

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:22.830
to catch future episodes
right when they come out.

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:25.395
Now go out there
and facet your data,

00:06:25.395 --> 00:06:29.502
and see how you can improve
your machine learning.

